feedback.body:
I suggest to restructure the fields as follows:
```
struct address {
    struct address *next;
    char addr[7];
    uint8_t ssid : 4;
    uint8_t commandHeard : 1;
    uint8_t __unused : 3;
}; ```

In this way the fields are properly aligned and there is no extra-cost due to padding

I suggest to a have this struct defined like this:
```
struct packet {
    struct packet *prev;
    struct packet *next;
    struct address addresses[N];
    datetime_t ts;
    char info[0];
}
```
This allows to allocate the entire struct with `malloc(sizeof(struct packet) + info_size);`, reducing memory fragmentation and malloc overhead.

See also https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Zero-Length.html

These functions are dealing with packet lists, more than with single packets. Additionally, we have a packet list both in the radio state data structure and in the rtx status.
I suggest to do the following:
- create a new data structure representing a list of packets
```
struct aprsPktList {
    struct aprsPacket *packets;
    size_t nbPackets;
}
```
- rename the functions to be `aprsPktList_insert`, `aprsPktList_free`, `aprsPktList_delete`,...

Eventually, I'd split the file in two translation units: one named `packet.h/.c` which contains the definition of the `aprsPacket` struct and functions to do the packet <> raw frame conversion, and one named `packet_list.h/.c` which contains only the list implementation.

